#!/usr/bin/env perl

package Trog::Bin::Provisioner;

use 5.041;

use strict;
use warnings FATAL => 'all';

use re '/aasx';
use FindBin();
use FindBin::libs;
use Config::Simple();
use Trog::HV();
use Trog::Local();
use Trog::Hypervisors();
use Trog::Guest();
use Trog::Config();
use Trog::Credentials();
use Trog::Secrets();
use Provisioner::Cookbook();
use Provisioner::Utils();
use File::Basename();
use File::Slurper();
use File::Slurper::Temp();
use File::Temp();
use Provisioner::IPPool();
use YAML::XS();
use List::Util   qw{uniq};
use Getopt::Long qw{GetOptionsFromArray};
use Pod::Usage   qw{pod2usage};
use Trog::Utils();

use Carp::Always;

=head1 NAME

provision - build a VM from a domain directory

=head1 SYNOPSIS

    provision [OPTIONS] DOMAIN

    provision vm.example.com
    provision --connect qemu+ssh://root@hv1.example.net/system vm.example.com

=head1 OPTIONS

=over 4

=item B<--connect> URI

The libvirt connection string of the hypervisor to build on.  With this option,
provision does not read F<hypervisors.conf>.  It does not choose a hypervisor
and does not look at capacity.  It builds the guest on this one.  A remote
hypervisor must use an ssh transport (C<qemu+ssh://user@host/system>), because
provision needs its filesystem as well as its libvirt.

=item B<--credentials>

Reads the passwords that the run needs from standard input before the run
starts, and does not ask for them.  Use it for a run with no terminal, for
example the reprovision button of tCMS, or cron.  See L<Trog::Credentials> for
the format.

=item B<--no-config>

Does not generate the configuration of the domain, and builds from the files
that are already in its directory.  Without this option, provision runs
F<bin/new_config> first.  That program writes F<provision.conf> and packs
F<data.tar.gz>.  Use this option to build exactly what is on disk.

=item B<--salvage-gaps-ok>

Rebuilds the guest even when state that is on the guest did not come off it.

Before a rebuild, the salvage copies the state of each service off the guest.
If the salvage finds a directory on the guest and gets nothing from it, the run
stops before it destroys anything.  That is what a directory that cannot be read
looks like, and a rebuild loses the only copy.  Use this option when the state
is really gone, or when you do not want it, and the guest must be rebuilt
anyway.

This option exists only on the command line.  No environment variable and no
configuration key sets it, because the operator must read what the run is
about to lose.

A directory that the guest never wrote does not stop the run.  Neither does a
domain that has no guest yet.  So a new machine never needs this option.

=item B<--clone-on-nonreusable>

Copies the disk of the guest to C<DOMAIN.bak-qcow2> before the rebuild destroys
it, and then rebuilds.

The copy is a disk and nothing else.  No domain is defined for it, it gets no
address, and nothing starts it.  Nothing removes it later, and that includes
F<bin/destroy>.  To remove these copies, run C<bin/destroy --backups>.

If provision cannot make the copy, it does not rebuild.

You can pass only one of the three C<--*-on-nonreusable> options.

=item B<--destroy-on-nonreusable>

Rebuilds the guest even though the rebuild destroys it.

A rebuild usually keeps the disk of the guest.  If this build asks for a size
or layout that is different from the disk, the disk cannot be kept.  Then the
domain is undefined and the disk is deleted.  Without this option, provision
stops and asks first.  If there is no terminal to ask at, it refuses.

You can pass only one of the three C<--*-on-nonreusable> options.

=item B<--die-on-nonreusable>

Refuses such a rebuild, and does not ask.

provision does this anyway when nobody is there to ask.  Pass this option to
say so on purpose, and to be sure of it where a terminal can exist.

You can pass only one of the three C<--*-on-nonreusable> options.

=item B<--recipes> FILE

Passed to F<bin/new_config>.  The default is
F</etc/trog-provisioner/recipes.yaml>.

=item B<--ipmap> FILE

Passed to F<bin/new_config>.  The default is F</etc/trog-provisioner/ipmap.cfg>.

=item B<--hvconf> FILE

The fleet definition, which is the file that lists the hypervisors.  The
default is F</etc/trog-provisioner/hypervisors.conf>.  If the file exists,
provision chooses the hypervisor from it.  See L<Trog::Hypervisors>.  If it
does not exist, F<provision.conf> and C<--connect> give the hypervisor.  For
most people, that is still this machine.

=item B<--domaindir> DIR

The directory that holds the directory of each domain.  Without this option,
the hypervisor gives the directory, and the default is F</opt/domains>.

=item B<--existing> IP

Adds the configuration of this domain to a VM that already runs at IP, and does
not build a new one.

=item B<--dryrun>

Writes the configuration of the domain, and changes nothing in any other place.

F<network-config>, F<user-data>, F<meta-data> and F<setup.sh> go into the
domain directory as usual.  provision describes each step outside that
directory, and does not do it.  It makes no disk and no cloud-init seed.  It
does not destroy or define the domain.  It does not change the
C<authorized_keys> of this machine.

A domain that already has a key keeps it.  The guest that is up has the public
half.  If provision replaces the private half here, it loses the way in to a
machine that the dry run does not touch.  A domain with no key gets one,
because F<user-data> is written from it.

A dry run does not make F<domain.xml>.  That file names the storage pool, the
base image, the disk of the guest and its seed.  To write the file, provision
must make all four, and a dry run must not make them.

=back

=head1 DESCRIPTION

provision builds the guest for DOMAIN and runs its makefile.  First it runs
F<bin/new_config> to generate the configuration of the domain, unless you pass
C<--no-config>.  Then it reads F<DOMAIN/provision.conf> and
F<DOMAIN/users.yaml> in the domain directory, chooses the hypervisor, and asks
the hypervisor to build the guest.  Last, it puts the secrets onto the guest,
and waits for cloud-init and the makefile to finish.

If F<provision.conf> names another domain in C<depends_on>, provision builds
the guest of that domain first, and adds DOMAIN to it.  See L<Trog::HV> for what
"the hypervisor" means when it is not this machine.

=cut

sub _coerce_array (@values) {
    return \@values;
}

# The settings that are lists.  Config::Simple returns a string when the file
# names one value, and an array reference when it names two.  Every recipe
# expects an array, so without this a guest with one resolver is refused.
my %LIST_SETTING = map { $_ => 1 } qw{resolvers ips packages transfer_ips};

=head2 %settings = _settings($config)

Returns all that F<provision.conf> says, as a plain hash, to give to a recipe.
$config is the Config::Simple object of the file.  Each setting in
C<%LIST_SETTING> is an array reference.

Config::Simple puts the name of its block in front of each key.
F<provision.conf> has no blocks, so a key comes back as C<default.image>, and a
recipe that asks for C<image> does not find it.  This sub removes the prefix.
It does not ask for the block by name.  C<< param(-block => 'default') >> works
only on a file that Config::Simple reads as INI syntax.  It decides that from a
[header] line, and F<provision.conf> has none.

=cut

sub _settings {
    my ($config) = @_;

    my %vars     = $config->vars();
    my %settings = map { (s/\Adefault[.]//r) => $vars{$_} } keys %vars;

    $settings{$_} = Provisioner::Utils::coerce_arrayref( $settings{$_} )
      for grep { exists $settings{$_} } keys %LIST_SETTING;

    return %settings;
}

#TODO: CPU and GPU passthrough acceleration, with techniques such as this one:
# https://github.com/HarbourHeading/KVM-GPU-Passthrough

=head2 $fh = _must_read($path, $what)

Opens $path for reading and returns the handle.  If the open fails, it dies
with C<$what: $path> and the reason.

The open is the test.  A C<-f> test is true or false at a moment that is over
when the code acts on it.  It also cannot tell a missing file from a file that
this user cannot read.  This tool meets the second case, because it can run as
a user that does not own the domain directory.  An open answers both
questions, and the answer stays true while the handle is in use.

=cut

sub _must_read {
    my ( $path, $what ) = @_;
    open( my $fh, '<', $path ) or die "$what: $path ($!)\n";
    return $fh;
}

# Each domain gets its secrets once in a run.  On the reuse path,
# reprovision_guest places them and schedules the makefile, and then main
# places them again.  A second write replaces a key that the recipe already
# gave to the service that reads it.
my %PLACED;

=head2 place_guest_secrets($guest, $domain)

Copies the files that a recipe reads, but must never generate, from the secret
store onto the guest.  It does this before the makefile that reads them
starts.

F<bin/new_config> writes a manifest, F<guest-secrets.yaml>, in the domain
directory.  For each file, the manifest gives the path on the guest and the
reference in the store that holds the value.  It holds the reference and never
the value.  So nobody can read a secret from the domain directory or from a
backup of it.

Most guests ask for no secret, and F<bin/new_config> writes no manifest for
them.  C<--no-config> writes no manifest at all.  With no manifest, this sub
does nothing and returns an empty list.  It also returns an empty list on a
second call for the same domain.  It returns 1 after it places the files and
calls C<authorize_runner_key>.

It asks for the password of the store through L<Trog::Credentials>.  It dies if
it cannot open the store, if the store has no value for a reference, or if it
cannot write a file to the guest.  A guest that does not get its signing key, or
a similar secret, makes a new identity for itself.  This sub exists to stop
that.  So it fails here, where an operator watches, and not three minutes later
on the guest.

=cut

sub place_guest_secrets {
    my ( $guest, $domain ) = @_;

    return if $PLACED{$domain}++;

    # The domain directory of the hypervisor of this run, which is the
    # directory that bin/destroy purges.
    my $manifest = Trog::HV->new()->domain_dir . "/$domain/guest-secrets.yaml";

    return unless -e $manifest;

    my $wanted = YAML::XS::Load( File::Slurper::read_binary($manifest) );
    return unless ref $wanted eq 'HASH' && %$wanted;

    my %needed = map { $_ => $wanted->{$_}{ref} } keys %$wanted;
    my %values = Trog::Secrets->lookup( Trog::Config->path('secrets.kdbx'), Trog::Credentials->prompt( 'Enter password:', 'keepass' ), %needed );

    foreach my $path ( sort keys %$wanted ) {
        my $value = $values{$path};
        die "The secret store had nothing for $wanted->{$path}{ref}, which $domain needs at $path\n"
          unless length $value;    ## no critic (ValuesAndExpressions::ProhibitDefinedBeforeLength) -- a secret of "0" is still a secret

        # The recipe makes this directory later.  This runs first, so the
        # directory is not there yet, and a write into it fails.
        my $dir = File::Basename::dirname($path);
        die "Could not make $dir on $domain\n" if $guest->run_sudo( 'mkdir', '-p', $dir );

        $guest->write_text( $path, "$value\n", sudo => 1, mode => $wanted->{$path}{mode} // '0600' )
          or die "Could not place $path on $domain\n";

        # Try the owner, but do not require it.  The account is usually from a
        # package that the makefile has not installed yet.  Until the recipe
        # changes the owner, the mode above keeps the secret private.  That is
        # why guest_secrets gives the final owner, not the first one.
        my $owner = $wanted->{$path}{owner} // 'root:root';
        warn "$path is root's for now: no $owner on $domain yet, so the recipe has to give it away\n"
          if $guest->run_sudo( 'chown', $owner, $path );

        print "Placed $path on $domain from $wanted->{$path}{ref}\n";
    }

    authorize_runner_key( $domain, \%values );
    return 1;
}

=head2 authorize_runner_key($domain, $values)

Adds the key of a runner to the F<authorized_keys> of each hypervisor that the
runner builds guests on.  A runner is a guest that runs the C<trogrunner>
recipe.  $values is the hash reference, from path to value, that
C<place_guest_secrets> got from the store.

The change is to a machine that is not the guest, so the tool makes it and not
the recipe.  C<authorize_guest_key> is here for the same reason.  This sub is
not next to that one, because the key comes out of the store here.  In any
other place, the operator must type the password of the store a second time.

L<Provisioner::Cookbook> tells which hypervisors get the key.  This sub does
nothing and returns 0 for a domain that does not run C<trogrunner>, or that
keeps the default C<hypervisor_access> of C<none>.  It gives a warning and
returns 0 for a domain that asks for access and names no hypervisor.  It
returns 1 when it added the key.

It writes the public half beside the domain as F<hypervisor-key.pub>.  The file
is public, and F<bin/destroy> reads it to remove the key without the password
of the store.

It dies if the store has no private key for the domain, if the value is not a
key, if a hypervisor has no C<libvirt_uri>, or if it cannot add the key.

A dry run never gets here, because C<provision_domain> returns before it
places any secret.

=cut

sub authorize_runner_key {
    my ( $domain, $values ) = @_;

    my $config = eval { Provisioner::Cookbook->domain_config($domain) } // {};
    my %grant  = Provisioner::Cookbook->load('trogrunner')->grant( $config->{trogrunner} );
    return 0 unless %grant;

    my $hypervisors = $grant{hypervisors};
    unless (%$hypervisors) {
        warn "$domain asked for $grant{access} on its hypervisors and names none, so there is nobody to authorize it with\n";
        return 0;
    }

    my $domain_dir = Trog::HV->new()->domain_dir;
    my ($private) = grep { m{/\.ssh/id_ed25519\z} } keys %$values;
    die "$domain wants hypervisor access and the store had no key for it\n"
      unless $private && length( $values->{$private} // q{} );    ## no critic (ValuesAndExpressions::ProhibitDefinedBeforeLength) -- a key of "0" is still a key

    my $pubkey = _public_half( $domain, $values->{$private} );
    File::Slurper::Temp::write_binary( "$domain_dir/$domain/hypervisor-key.pub", "$pubkey\n" );

    # from="<address>" when the domain holds one.  The address is stable,
    # because a new provision keeps the address of the domain.  Only
    # bin/destroy gives it back.
    my $line = $pubkey;
    if ( $grant{restrict} ) {
        my $ip = eval { Provisioner::IPPool::held_by($domain) };
        $line = qq{from="$ip" $pubkey} if $ip;
    }

    foreach my $name ( sort keys %$hypervisors ) {
        my $uri = $hypervisors->{$name}{libvirt_uri}
          or die "$domain names a hypervisor '$name' with no libvirt_uri\n";

        my $machine = Trog::HV->candidate( uri => $uri );
        $machine->append_line( $machine->authorized_keys, $line )
          or die "Could not authorize $domain on $name (" . $machine->describe . ")\n";

        print "Authorized $domain on $name as " . ( $machine->ssh_user // 'the connecting user' ) . "\n";
    }

    print "$domain now has $grant{access} on: " . join( ', ', sort keys %$hypervisors ) . "\n";
    return 1;
}

=head2 $pubkey = _public_half($domain, $private)

Returns the public half of the private key $private, followed by the comment
C<trog-provisioner runner $domain>.  It dies if
C<Provisioner::Utils::ssh_pubkey_from_private> cannot read $private as a key.

That function reads a key from a file, so this sub first writes $private to a
temporary file with mode 0600.

=cut

sub _public_half {
    my ( $domain, $private ) = @_;

    my $tmp = File::Temp->new( UNLINK => 1 );
    chmod 0600, $tmp->filename;
    print {$tmp} "$private\n";
    $tmp->flush();

    my $pubkey = eval { Provisioner::Utils::ssh_pubkey_from_private( $tmp->filename ) };
    die "Could not read the public half of $domain's hypervisor key; the store may be holding something that is not one.\n$@"
      unless $pubkey;

    # The comment names the line, so that a reader of authorized_keys can tell
    # whose it is and remove it by hand.
    return "$pubkey trog-provisioner runner $domain";
}

# What to do about a rebuild that destroys the guest, as the
# --*-on-nonreusable options set it.  `ask` means that no option gave an
# answer.  It is not a fourth action.
our %NONREUSABLE = (
    ask     => 0,
    clone   => 1,
    destroy => 2,
    stop    => 3,
);

sub main {
    my @args = @_;

    umask 0022;

    my ( $reuse, $uri, $domain_dir, $hvconf, $no_config, $recipes, $ipmap, $credentials, $salvage_gaps_ok, $dryrun );
    my ( $clone_on_nonreusable, $destroy_on_nonreusable, $die_on_nonreusable );
    GetOptionsFromArray(
        \@args,
        'existing=s'             => \$reuse,
        'domaindir=s'            => \$domain_dir,
        'dryrun'                 => \$dryrun,
        'connect=s'              => \$uri,
        'hvconf=s'               => \$hvconf,
        'no-config'              => \$no_config,
        'recipes=s'              => \$recipes,
        'ipmap=s'                => \$ipmap,
        'credentials'            => \$credentials,
        'salvage-gaps-ok'        => \$salvage_gaps_ok,
        'clone-on-nonreusable'   => \$clone_on_nonreusable,
        'destroy-on-nonreusable' => \$destroy_on_nonreusable,
        'die-on-nonreusable'     => \$die_on_nonreusable,
    );

    my @said = grep { $_ } ( $clone_on_nonreusable, $destroy_on_nonreusable, $die_on_nonreusable );
    pod2usage(
        -exitval => 2, -verbose => 1, -input => __FILE__,
        -message => 'the --*-on-nonreusable options say different things; pass one'
    ) if scalar @said > 1;

    my $nonreusable = $NONREUSABLE{ask};
    $nonreusable = $NONREUSABLE{clone}   if $clone_on_nonreusable;
    $nonreusable = $NONREUSABLE{destroy} if $destroy_on_nonreusable;
    $nonreusable = $NONREUSABLE{stop}    if $die_on_nonreusable;

    # Before anything asks for a password, and only with --credentials.  See
    # Trog::Credentials, which reads no handle that it was not given.
    Trog::Credentials->load() if $credentials;

    my $domain = shift @args;
    pod2usage( -exitval => 2, -verbose => 1, -input => __FILE__, -message => 'No domain passed' )
      unless $domain;

    # The choice of hypervisor reads provision.conf, so find that file first.
    # Do not ask Trog::HV for an object here, because that fixes the singleton
    # before generate_config chooses the hypervisor.  The class gives the
    # default without an object, so the default is in one place.
    #
    # Keep the value from the command line.  After the default, a given value
    # and a default look the same, and only a given value beats the hypervisor.
    my $domain_dir_from_cli = $domain_dir;
    $domain_dir //= Trog::HV->default_domain_dir;
    $domain =~ s/\///g;

    # Generate the configuration of the domain first.  It writes provision.conf
    # and packs data.tar.gz, and it needs the hypervisor to write them.  So it
    # also chooses the hypervisor, and all the code after it uses that choice.
    generate_config(
        $domain,
        {
            domain_dir => $domain_dir,
            recipes    => $recipes,
            ipmap      => $ipmap,
            hvconf     => $hvconf,
            uri        => $uri,
            dryrun     => $dryrun,

            # Only from the command line: see --salvage-gaps-ok in the POD.
            salvage_gaps_ok => $salvage_gaps_ok,
        }
    ) unless $no_config;

    # Ask the hypervisor that generate_config chose, because its block in
    # hypervisors.conf can set the domain directory.
    $domain_dir = Trog::HV->new()->domain_dir
      unless $no_config || defined $domain_dir_from_cli;

    my $cfile = "$domain_dir/$domain/provision.conf";
    close( _must_read( $cfile, "No provision.conf for $domain" ) ) or die "Could not close $cfile: $!\n";

    my $udf = "$domain_dir/$domain/users.yaml";
    close( _must_read( $udf, "No users.yaml for $domain" ) ) or die "Could not close $udf: $!\n";

    my $config = Config::Simple->new($cfile);
    my $reuser = $config->param('admin_user') // 'root';

    # Choose the hypervisor before anything changes.  Every other sub gets the
    # same object back from Trog::HV->new().
    my $hv = choose_hypervisor(
        $config, $domain,
        {
            uri        => $uri,
            domain_dir => $domain_dir,
            hvconf     => $hvconf,
        }
    );
    print "Provisioning against hypervisor " . $hv->uri . "\n" unless $hv->is_local;

    $domain_dir = $hv->domain_dir;
    my $data = "$domain_dir/$domain/data.tar.gz";
    close( _must_read( $data, "No data to host for $domain" ) ) or die "Could not close $data: $!\n";

    # Its storage pool and the virtiofs shim, or nothing on a hypervisor that
    # has neither.
    $hv->prepare_host("$FindBin::Bin/../virtiofs-better");

    # If this domain depends on another, build that guest first.  With
    # --existing, this only adds configuration to a guest that is up.
    my $skey;
    my $depends = $config->param('depends_on');
    if ($depends) {
        print "$domain depends on $depends, provisioning that first...\n";
        my $dcfile = "$domain_dir/$depends/provision.conf";
        close( _must_read( $dcfile, "No provision.conf for $depends, which $domain depends on" ) ) or die "Could not close $dcfile: $!\n";
        my $dconfig = Config::Simple->new($dcfile);

        #XXX Perhaps die here if the admin_user settings differ.

        ( $reuser, $reuse ) = provision_domain( config => $dconfig, domain => $depends, reuse => $reuse, reuser => $reuser, dryrun => $dryrun, nonreusable => $nonreusable );

        # After provision_domain, because it makes this key and puts it in the
        # store.  Before it, this gets the key of the old guest.
        $skey = Trog::Guest->key_path( $depends, "$domain_dir/$depends/key.rsa" );
        if ( !$dryrun ) {
            my $guest = Trog::Guest->new( name => $depends, host => $reuse, user => $reuser, key_path => $skey );
            $guest->wait_for_ssh();
            $guest->wait_for_cloud_init($depends);
            $hv->release_seed($depends);
            $guest->wait_for_makefile($depends)
              or die "The build for $depends failed; read /var/log/$depends.setup.log on the guest\n";
        }

    }

    #TODO: Find how to apply the cloud-init of the second domain when it has a dependency.
    my ( $user, $ip ) = provision_domain( config => $config, domain => $domain, reuse => $reuse, reuser => $reuser, depends => $depends, dryrun => $dryrun, nonreusable => $nonreusable );
    return 0 if $dryrun;

    # After provision_domain, for the reason above.  A domain with a dependency
    # already has the key of that guest.
    $skey //= Trog::Guest->key_path( $domain, "$domain_dir/$domain/key.rsa" );

    # --out, not a $(...) substitution.  Without --out, bin/guest_key writes a
    # temporary file and removes it when it exits.  So ssh gets a path that is
    # already gone.
    print "bin/guest_key --out $domain.key $domain && ssh -i $domain.key $user\@$ip\n";
    my $guest = Trog::Guest->new( name => $domain, host => $ip, user => $user, key_path => $skey );
    $guest->wait_for_ssh();

    my $map = "$domain_dir/$domain/devices.map";
    if ( -f $map ) {    ## no critic (ValuesAndExpressions::ProhibitFiletest_f) -- whether there is one to send; put_file dies if it has gone since
        print "Syncing disk map\n";
        $guest->put_file( $map, '/root/devices.map', sudo => 1 )
          or die "could not sync devices map!";
    }

    # Before cloud-init finishes, so before the makefile that it schedules
    # starts.  A recipe reads these files and does not generate them.
    place_guest_secrets( $guest, $domain );

    $guest->wait_for_cloud_init($domain);

    # Only now.  cloud-init reads the seed ISO until it finishes, and without
    # the ISO the guest gets no user, no keys and no netplan.  The ISO comes
    # out only so that the guest does not boot from it next time.
    $hv->release_seed($domain);

    $guest->wait_for_makefile($domain)
      or die "The build for $domain failed; read /var/log/$domain.setup.log on the guest\n";

    return 0;
}

=head2 generate_config($domain, $opts)

Runs F<bin/new_config> for $domain, in this process, when there are recipes
for it to read.  $opts is a hash reference.  This sub reads its keys
C<domain_dir>, C<recipes>, C<ipmap>, C<uri>, C<dryrun> and C<salvage_gaps_ok>.
C<recipes> and C<ipmap> default to the files that L<Trog::Config> names.

It returns 0 and does nothing when there is no F<recipes.yaml> and no
F<recipes.d> beside it.  Then somebody provisions from a domain directory that
they wrote by hand, which is supported.  It returns 1 after it generates the
configuration.

With C<uri>, this sub sets the hypervisor before the generator runs, and the
generator uses it.  Without C<uri>, the generator chooses the hypervisor.  In
both cases, the rest of this program uses the same one.

It dies if the generator fails, and through C<refuse_on_salvage_gaps> if the
salvage left a gap.

=cut

sub generate_config {
    my ( $domain, $opts ) = @_;

    my $dir     = $opts->{domain_dir};
    my $recipes = $opts->{recipes} // Trog::Config->path('recipes.yaml');
    my $ipmap   = $opts->{ipmap}   // Trog::Config->path('ipmap.cfg');

    my $recipe_dir = File::Basename::dirname($recipes) . '/recipes.d';
    unless ( -f $recipes || -d $recipe_dir ) {    ## no critic (ValuesAndExpressions::ProhibitFiletest_f) -- whether there is site data at all; the generator opens what there is
        print "No recipes at $recipes, building from what is already in $dir/$domain\n";
        return 0;
    }

    print "Generating the configuration for $domain...\n";

    # The generator takes this singleton, and does not choose a hypervisor.
    Trog::HV->new( uri => $opts->{uri}, domain_dir => $dir ) if $opts->{uri};

    push @INC, '.';
    my $generator = File::Basename::dirname(__FILE__) . '/new_config';
    require $generator;
    my @flags = ( '--recipes', $recipes, '--ipmap', $ipmap );

    # For the generator, this only keeps the key of a guest that is up.  See
    # DRY RUNS in bin/new_config.
    push( @flags, '--dryrun' ) if $opts->{dryrun};

    my $result = Trog::Provisioner::Config::Generator::main( @flags, $domain );

    die "Could not generate the configuration for $domain\n" if $result;

    refuse_on_salvage_gaps( $opts->{salvage_gaps_ok} );
    return 1;
}

=head2 refuse_on_salvage_gaps($allowed)

Stops the run before anything is destroyed when the salvage did not get state
that is still on the guest.  To salvage is to copy the state of a service off
the guest before the rebuild.

It returns 1 when there is no gap.  With a true $allowed, it gives a warning
that lists the gaps and returns 1.  Otherwise it dies with the list.

The generator only gives a warning, and that is correct for it.  It writes
configuration, and it runs from cron to take backups.  There, a stop for one
directory that it cannot read abandons every domain after it.  This program
asks a hypervisor to C<clear_guest>, so here the warning becomes a refusal.

It counts only a gap where the guest still has files that did not come off it.
A path with nothing under it has nothing to salvage, and every first build of a
recipe looks like that.  A domain with no guest is not salvaged.  So a new
machine never stops here.

=cut

sub refuse_on_salvage_gaps {
    my ($allowed) = @_;

    my %gaps = Trog::Provisioner::Config::Generator::salvage_gaps();
    return 1 unless %gaps;

    my $detail = join(
        q{},
        map {
            my $domain = $_;
            map { "    $domain: $_->{recipe} read nothing out of $_->{remote}\n" } @{ $gaps{$domain} }
        } sort keys %gaps
    );

    if ($allowed) {
        warn "Continuing past " . scalar( keys %gaps ) . " domain(s) whose state could not be read, because --salvage-gaps-ok says to:\n" . $detail;
        return 1;
    }

    die "Refusing to rebuild: state that is on the guest did not come off it.\n\n" . $detail
      . "\nThe guest was asked, and it still has files under each of those -- so this is\na fetch that did not bring them, not a service that has nothing.  Rebuilding now\ndestroys the only copy.\n\nThe salvage reads them as root over sudo, so the usual cause is sudo being\nrefused on the guest or the fetch failing partway.  Look at what it said above,\nfix it and run again, or pass --salvage-gaps-ok if that state is gone for good\nand you want the guest rebuilt without it.\n";
}

=head2 $hv = choose_hypervisor($config, $domain, $opts)

Returns the hypervisor that the guest of $domain belongs on.  $config is the
Config::Simple object of F<provision.conf>.  $opts is a hash reference with the
keys C<uri>, C<domain_dir> and C<hvconf>.

With C<uri>, from C<--connect>, it returns that hypervisor and asks nothing
else.  Otherwise, a configured F<hypervisors.conf> decides.  The guest goes
where it is now, or where it fits.  See L<Trog::Hypervisors>.  With no fleet,
F<provision.conf> holds the connection details.

=cut

sub choose_hypervisor {
    my ( $config, $domain, $opts ) = @_;

    my %paths = ( domain_dir => $opts->{domain_dir} );

    return Trog::HV->new( uri => $opts->{uri}, %paths ) if $opts->{uri};

    my $fleet = Trog::Hypervisors->load( $opts->{hvconf} // Trog::Hypervisors->default_path );
    return Trog::HV->from_config( $config, %paths ) unless $fleet->configured;

    warn "hypervisors.conf decides which hypervisor $domain lands on; the " . "libvirt_uri in its provision.conf is ignored\n"
      if $config->param('libvirt_uri');

    my $hv = $fleet->select_for( $domain, $config );

    # The command line beats the fleet on where our files go.
    $hv->{domain_dir} = $opts->{domain_dir} if defined $opts->{domain_dir};
    return $hv;
}

=head2 $behavior = ask_about_nonreusable($domain)

Asks the operator what to do about a rebuild that destroys the guest of
$domain.  Call it only when a person is there to answer.

It returns C<$NONREUSABLE{clone}> for the answer C<copy>, and
C<$NONREUSABLE{destroy}> for C<destroy>.  For any other answer, an empty one
too, it returns C<$NONREUSABLE{stop}>.

=cut

sub ask_about_nonreusable {
    my ($domain) = @_;

    print "$domain is already there, and its disk cannot be kept at the size this build asks for.\n" . "Rebuilding destroys the guest and everything on it.\n";

    my $answer = Trog::Utils::prompt('Copy the disk aside first, destroy it and rebuild, or stop? [copy/destroy/stop]:');

    return $NONREUSABLE{clone}   if ( lc "$answer" ) eq 'copy';
    return $NONREUSABLE{destroy} if ( lc "$answer" ) eq 'destroy';
    return $NONREUSABLE{stop};
}

sub provision_domain (%args) {
    my ( $config, $domain, $reuse, $reuser, $depends, $dryrun, $nonreusable ) = @args{qw{config domain reuse reuser depends dryrun nonreusable}};

    my $hv = Trog::HV->new();

    # read_seed and authorize_guest_key read the domain from $config.
    $config->param( 'domain', $domain );

    # A dry run stops here.  It has no ISO for the seed and no machine to tell
    # about it, so no code below reads the seed or adds a key anywhere.
    # would_provision describes the build and returns where the guest is, or
    # where it is going to be.  This does not ask guest_ssh_ip, because main()
    # discards the address on a dry run.  For a guest that does not exist, a
    # cloud looks up the name and dies, and libvirt returns the placeholder.
    if ($dryrun) {
        return ( $reuser,                      $reuse ) if $reuse;
        return ( $config->param('admin_user'), $hv->would_provision( $config, reuse => $reuse ) );
    }

    return reprovision_guest( hv => $hv, config => $config, domain => $domain, reuse => $reuse, reuser => $reuser, depends => $depends ) if $reuse;

    # The guest fetches its payload from this machine, so let its key in.
    my %seed = read_seed($config);
    authorize_guest_key($config);

    my %settings = _settings($config);

    if ( $hv->rebuild_destroys_guest( $domain, capacity => $settings{size} ) ) {
        my $behavior = $nonreusable // $NONREUSABLE{ask};

        # With no terminal, stop.  A prompt that nobody answers hangs the run,
        # and make and agents run this.
        $behavior = Trog::Local->interactive ? ask_about_nonreusable($domain) : $NONREUSABLE{stop}
          if $behavior == $NONREUSABLE{ask};

        die "Refusing to rebuild $domain: it is already there, and its disk cannot be kept at the size this build asks for.\n" . "Rebuilding would destroy the guest and everything on it.\n" . "Pass --clone-on-nonreusable to copy the disk aside first, or --destroy-on-nonreusable to do it anyway.\n"
          if $behavior == $NONREUSABLE{stop};

        if ( $behavior == $NONREUSABLE{clone} ) {
            my $copy = $hv->clone_guest_disk($domain)
              or die "Could not copy $domain's disk aside, so nothing here will rebuild over it.\n";
            print "Copied $domain's disk aside as $copy; nothing removes that, including bin/destroy.\n";
        }

        print "Rebuilding $domain destroys it: its disk cannot be kept at the size this build asks for.\n";
    }

    # Take a snapshot before the rebuild, when there is a guest to go back to.
    # A first build has no guest and nothing to lose.  A rebuild that cannot
    # keep the disk deletes it, so a snapshot in it has no use.  The backend
    # decides which case this is, and what a snapshot needs.
    my $rollback = $hv->snapshot_before_rebuild( $domain, capacity => $settings{size} );
    print "Snapshotted $domain as $rollback before rebuilding it.\n" . "If this goes badly: bin/restore --name $rollback $domain\n" if $rollback;

    # The backend decides what each step does.  libvirt removes a domain and
    # its disks, and a cloud rebuilds the server that it has.  This sub gives
    # the order, and asks the backend to do each step.
    #
    # keep_disk when there is a rollback point, because on libvirt that point
    # is in the disk.  If the disk goes, the snapshot goes with it.
    $hv->clear_guest( $domain, keep_disk => !!$rollback );

    my $address = $hv->provision_guest( $config, \%seed, settings => \%settings, reuse => $reuse );

    return ( $config->param('admin_user'), $hv->guest_ssh_ip( $config, $address ) );
}

=head2 ($user, $host) = reprovision_guest(%args)

Adds a domain to a guest that is up, and does not build a guest.  It takes the
keys C<hv>, C<config>, C<domain>, C<reuse>, C<reuser> and C<depends>.  It
returns C<reuser> and C<reuse>, the user and the address to connect with.

The machine is C<reuse>.  That is the address from C<--existing>, or the guest
that was built for the dependency.  This is how a shared host works: bar.test
goes onto the guest of foo.test, and does not get its own.  Only two things
here come from the hypervisor.  One is the MAC of the second interface of the
guest.  The other is the removal of a guest that has the name of this domain.

It ships F<user-data>, the addresses, F<setup.sh> and the secrets, and
schedules F<setup.sh> with C<at>.  It dies if it cannot ship a file.

=cut

sub reprovision_guest (%args) {
    my ( $hv, $config, $domain, $reuse, $reuser, $depends ) = @args{qw{hv config domain reuse reuser depends}};

    # The machine belongs to $built_for.  With a dependency, that is the
    # domain that was built, and its key opens the machine.  Without one, this
    # domain is the guest.  With no key, ssh uses the agent.  $reuser is root
    # when no admin user is named, and a locked-down machine can refuse root.
    my $built_for  = $depends // $domain;
    my $domain_dir = $hv->domain_dir;
    my $key        = Trog::Guest->key_path( $built_for, "$domain_dir/$built_for/key.rsa" );

    # With a dependency, remove any guest that still has the name of this
    # domain.  That is the guest this domain had before it moved to the shared
    # one.  Without a dependency, that name is the machine this connects to.
    $hv->clear_guest($domain) if $depends;

    print "Attempting to access Machine\n";
    my $guest = Trog::Guest->new( name => $domain, host => $reuse, user => $reuser, key_path => $key );

    refresh_cloud_init( $guest, $domain_dir, $domain );
    merge_guest_addresses( guest => $guest, hv => $hv, config => $config, domain_dir => $domain_dir, domain => $domain, built_for => $built_for );

    print "Syncing setup script\n";
    $guest->put_file( "$domain_dir/$domain/setup.sh", "/root/setup-$domain.sh", sudo => 1, mode => '0755' )
      or die "Could not ship the setup script to $domain";

    place_guest_secrets( $guest, $domain );

    print "Scheduling job to run setup script\n" . $guest->capture_cmd("sudo at now -f /root/setup-$domain.sh") . "Done.\n";

    return ( $reuser, $reuse );
}

=head2 refresh_cloud_init($guest, $domain_dir, $domain)

Ships the F<user-data> of $domain to the guest, and runs the cloud-init modules
C<cc_package_update_upgrade_install> and C<cc_users_groups> again.  It returns
1, and dies if it cannot ship the file.

cloud-init runs once and remembers that it ran.  A module that you name runs
again anyway.  That applies the new configuration to a guest that booted from
an older one.

=cut

sub refresh_cloud_init {
    my ( $guest, $domain_dir, $domain ) = @_;

    print "Installing Updated cloud-init and forcing re-run...\n";
    my $init = "$domain_dir/$domain/user-data";
    $guest->put_file( $init, 'cloud-config.txt' ) or die "Could not ship the cloud-init config to $domain";

    foreach my $module (qw{cc_package_update_upgrade_install cc_users_groups}) {
        print $guest->capture_cmd("sudo cloud-init single --name $module -f cloud-config.txt") . "\n";
    }

    return 1;
}

=head2 merge_guest_addresses(%args)

Adds the addresses in the C<ips> setting of F<provision.conf> to the netplan of
the guest, and applies it.  It takes the keys C<guest>, C<hv>, C<config>,
C<domain_dir>, C<domain> and C<built_for>.  It returns 1.

It writes netplan only when the addresses change, because C<netplan apply> on
the same file restarts the network of the guest for no reason.

The interface belongs to C<built_for>, the domain that the machine was built
for.  The addresses belong to C<domain>.  On a shared host, these are two
different names.

It dies if it cannot install the new netplan file, and through
C<primary_adapter>.

=cut

sub merge_guest_addresses (%args) {
    my ( $guest, $hv, $config, $domain_dir, $domain, $built_for ) = @args{qw{guest hv config domain_dir domain built_for}};

    my $ips         = _coerce_array( $config->param('ips') );
    my $existing_nc = $guest->capture_cmd('sudo cat /etc/netplan/50-cloud-init.yaml');
    my $parsed      = YAML::XS::Load($existing_nc);

    my $adapter   = primary_adapter( $parsed, $config, $hv->guest_mac( $built_for, 1 ) );
    my $addresses = ( $parsed->{network}{ethernets}{$adapter}{addresses} //= [] );

    my $num_ips = scalar @$addresses;
    @$addresses = uniq( @$addresses, map { "$_/24" } @$ips );
    return 1 if $num_ips == scalar @$addresses;

    my $new_config = "$domain_dir/$domain/network-config-merged.yaml";
    File::Slurper::Temp::write_text( $new_config, YAML::XS::Dump($parsed) );
    print "Setting up IP for $domain...\n";

    $guest->put_file( $new_config, '/etc/netplan/50-cloud-init.yaml', sudo => 1, mode => '0600' )
      or die "Could not install the netplan config on $domain";

    my $output = $guest->capture_cmd('sudo netplan apply');
    chomp $output;
    print "$output\n" if $output;

    return 1;
}

=head2 $name = primary_adapter($netplan, $config, $mac)

Returns the name of the outbound interface in $netplan, the parsed netplan of
the guest.  It finds the entry whose C<match> stanza has the MAC address $mac.

We choose the MAC, and the guest cannot change it.  cloud-init writes the MAC
that it matched into netplan, so the entry identifies itself.  The name of the
interface is only a result of the match.

For a guest whose netplan has no C<match> stanza, it uses the name.  That is
C<bridge_devname> from $config, or the bridge name from the hypervisor.

It dies if netplan has no C<ethernets> section, or if no entry has the MAC or
the name.

=cut

sub primary_adapter {
    my ( $netplan, $config, $mac ) = @_;

    my $ethernets = $netplan->{network}{ethernets};
    die "No ethernets at all in the guest's netplan config!\n" unless ref $ethernets eq 'HASH';

    foreach my $name ( sort keys %$ethernets ) {
        my $match = $ethernets->{$name}{match} or next;
        next unless ref $match eq 'HASH' && defined $match->{macaddress};
        return $name if lc $match->{macaddress} eq lc $mac;
    }

    my ( undef, $bridge_name ) = Trog::HV->new()->nic_names;
    my $named = $config->param('bridge_devname') // $bridge_name;
    return $named if ref $ethernets->{$named} eq 'HASH';

    die "Could not find the outbound adapter in the guest's netplan: nothing matches\n" . "$mac, and there is no $named either.  It has: " . join( ', ', sort keys %$ethernets ) . "\n";
}

=head2 %seed = read_seed($config)

Returns the three NoCloud files that the seed ISO of the guest is made from,
keyed by name: C<user-data>, C<meta-data> and C<network-config>.  It reads them
from the domain directory.

F<bin/new_config> writes them with the rest of the configuration of the domain.
They are the C<template_files> of the distro recipe.  It dies with the name of
the file if it cannot read one.  A guest cannot be built without its seed, so
there is no reason to continue.

A dry run does not call this, because it has no ISO to make and no guest to
give it to.

=cut

sub read_seed {
    my ($config) = @_;

    my $domain = $config->param('domain');
    my $dir    = Trog::HV->new()->domain_dir . "/$domain";
    my @files  = qw{user-data meta-data network-config};

    return map { $_ => scalar File::Slurper::read_text("$dir/$_") } @files;
}

=head2 authorize_guest_key($config)

Adds the public key of the guest to the F<authorized_keys> of this machine,
which holds the payload of the guest.  It returns 1.  It dies if it cannot read
F<key.rsa.pub> in the domain directory, or if it cannot add the line.

The change is to this machine and not to the domain directory.  That is why it
is here and not in the recipe that makes the key.  F<bin/destroy> removes the
key again.  It knows nothing about a key from a configuration run that never
became a guest.

A dry run never gets here, for the same reason as C<authorize_runner_key>.

=cut

sub authorize_guest_key {
    my ($config) = @_;

    my $domain = $config->param('domain');
    my $pubkey = File::Slurper::read_text( Trog::HV->new()->domain_dir . "/$domain/key.rsa.pub" );
    chomp $pubkey;

    my $this_machine = Trog::Local->new();
    $this_machine->append_line( $this_machine->authorized_keys, $pubkey )
      or die "Could not authorize the key for $domain on " . $this_machine->describe . "\n";
    return 1;
}

exit main(@ARGV) unless caller;

1;
